{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Rutland and Washington Railroad -- the slate-country route from Eagle Bridge, New York, northeast through Salem, Granville, West Pawlet and Poultney to Castleton and Rutland, Vermont. Chartered in Vermont November 13 1847 and built 1851-1852. East of Salem it ran on a perpetual rent-free lease of the Troy and Rutland Railroad's franchise (chartered in New York July 2 1849, lease obtained June 24 1850); the Troy and Rutland's own Salem-Eagle Bridge section opened in 1852 and was leased to the Rutland & Washington too, so both halves are drawn here as one route. After foreclosure sales in 1865-1867 the property passed to the Troy, Salem and Rutland Rail Road Company, which never operated it but leased and then merged into the Rensselaer and Saratoga Railroad on October 20 1868; the Delaware and Hudson leased the Rensselaer and Saratoga, including this line, on February 24 1870 and ran it as the Washington Branch. Its daily mixed train, the 'Slate Picker', is why the line stays on this map into the 1930s -- CLAUDE.md counts mixed trains as passenger service. Carried as three features sharing one geometry, split at each change of operator per Rule 4.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rutland_and_Washington_Railroad (chartered in Vermont November 13 1847; built 1851-1852 between Rutland and Eagle Bridge in Rensselaer County, New York; perpetual rent-free lease of Troy and Rutland franchise rights east of Salem obtained June 24 1850; Troy and Rutland chartered in New York July 2 1849, its Salem-Eagle Bridge section completed 1852 and leased to the Rutland and Washington; foreclosure sales 1865-1867 to the Troy, Salem and Rutland Rail Road Company, never an operating company, leased to and merged into the Rensselaer and Saratoga Railroad October 20 1868; Delaware and Hudson leased the Rensselaer and Saratoga including the Eagle Bridge-Rutland line February 24 1870)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware_and_Hudson_Rail_Trail (the corridor was the D&H's Washington Branch, trains known locally as the 'Slate Picker', running Eagle Bridge NY to Castleton VT; D&H abandoned Eagle Bridge-Salem in 1980; Poultney-Castleton and Rupert-Granville segments sold to the Vermont Agency of Transportation for the rail trail)", "https://railtrails.vermont.gov/trails/delaware-hudson-rail-trail/history-delaware-hudson-rail-trail/ (the line flourished around the turn of the twentieth century running the 'slate picker', a daily freight and passenger service handling 15-30 cars per trip, moving roofing slate from western Vermont quarries to eastern seaboard markets; passenger trains died off in the Depression years and the railroad continued as a freight route until 1980)"], "geometry_sources": ["Wikimedia Commons Data:Rutland and Washington Railroad.map (CC BY 4.0), derived from OpenStreetMap: its 'Troy & Rutland Rail Road' (Eagle Bridge-Salem, 27.57 km) and 'Rutland and Washington Rail-Road' (Salem-Rutland, 72.87 km) LineStrings chained at their shared Salem endpoint. Checked clean by the place-anchor artifact detector."], "not_yet_sourced": "Route mileage from a period source, to check the 62.4-mile traced length against. Service frequency before 1900. The June 24 1934 end-of-passenger-service date comes from a single secondary source; the Vermont Rail Trails history only says passenger trains 'died off in the Depression years'. Abandonment dates for the Salem-West Rupert and West Pawlet-Granville segments.", "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Rutland and Washington Railroad", "line": "Rutland and Washington: Eagle Bridge \u2013 Rutland", "id": "rw-eagle-bridge-rutland", "color": "#8E5572", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 40, "speed_source": "estimated: rural slate-country branch, mixed trains; unsourced", "geometry_quality": "Traced from OpenStreetMap-derived geometry, no straight-line approximation: 557 points over 100.4 km (62.4 miles), largest chord 2.44 km on the Eagle Bridge-Salem half and 1.63 km east of Salem. The last 18 km into Rutland runs alongside the Rutland Railroad's own line (rutland_green_mountain_flyer.geojson, western_vermont_bennington_rutland.geojson), which is why about 6.8 km of that approach falls within 800 m of track already on the map -- the two railroads genuinely shared the valley from Castleton in.", "segments": [{"start": 1852, "end": 1868, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Chartered in Vermont November 13 1847 and built 1851-1852 between Rutland and Eagle Bridge in Rensselaer County, New York. It ran east of Salem on a perpetual rent-free lease of the Troy and Rutland Railroad's franchise rights, obtained June 24 1850; the Troy and Rutland's own Salem-to-Eagle Bridge section was finished in 1852 and leased to the Rutland & Washington as well, so one company worked the whole route. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rutland_and_Washington_Railroad", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware_and_Hudson_Rail_Trail", "https://railtrails.vermont.gov/trails/delaware-hudson-rail-trail/history-delaware-hudson-rail-trail/"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-73.38794, 42.94986], [-73.38681