way_min": 720, "note": "The Delaware and Hudson leased the Rensselaer and Saratoga, this line among it, on February 24 1870, and worked it as the Washington Branch. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rutland_and_Washington_Railroad", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware_and_Hudson_Rail_Trail", "https://railtrails.vermont.gov/trails/delaware-hudson-rail-trail/history-delaware-hudson-rail-trail/"]}, {"start": 1900, "end": 1934, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The branch flourished around the turn of the century on Vermont slate: its daily mixed freight-and-passenger train, 15 to 30 cars, was known locally as 'the Slate Picker' and hauled roofing slate from the western Vermont quarries toward the eastern seaboard. Passenger trains died off in the Depression years, ending June 24 1934. Freight continued until 1980, when the D&H abandoned Eagle Bridge to Salem; the Poultney-Castleton and Rupert-Granville segments went to the Vermont Agency of Transportation and are now the Delaware and Hudson Rail Trail. The daily headway reflects the documented once-a-day mixed train; the exact figure is unsourced.", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rutland_and_Washington_Railroad", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware_and_Hudson_Rail_Trail", "https://railtrails.vermont.gov/trails/delaware-hudson-rail-trail/history-delaware-hudson-rail-trail/"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-73.38794, 42.94986], [-73.38681, 42.94995], [-73.38584, 42.9501], [-73.38517, 42.95024], [-73.38446, 42.95044], [-73.38359, 42.95073], [-73.38296, 42.95098], [-73.